    "case_summary": "A 48-year-old white man, a lifelong non-smoker with a remote bronchoscopic resection of a tracheal carcinoid 17 years earlier, presented with a three-month history of mild hemoptysis and cough without dyspnea. He also noted a non-tender, 2 × 2 cm subcutaneous mass on his left forearm without overlying skin changes. On examination, his vital signs were normal with an oxygen saturation of 94% on room air. Laboratory studies, including complete blood count, liver and renal function tests, and coagulation profile, were all within normal limits. A chest CT scan showed a polypoid lesion in the precarinal tracheal region adjacent to the anterior wall. Flexible bronchoscopy revealed a mass arising from the anterior tracheal wall just above the main carina, causing an 80% obstruction of the right main bronchus and 70% obstruction of the left main bronchus. The lesion was resected bronchoscopically with electrocautery, restoring full airway patency without complication. On hematoxylin–eosin staining, the tumor consisted of large nests of eosinophilic cells within a collagenous and myxoid matrix, and a carcinoid tumor was initially suspected.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Glomus tumor\nB. Carcinoid tumor\nC. Hamartomas and other benign airway neoplasms\nD. Infectious etiologies (mucus plugs, tuberculosis)\nE. Inflammatory diseases (sarcoidosis, Wegener disease, rheumatoid granuloma)\nF. Immunohistochemical profile of glomus tumor\nG. None\nH. None\n",
